    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Identical balls were placed in five identical test tubes as shown. Then water was added to each of the test tubes. The water level in test tubes 1, 2 and 3 is the same. The water level in test tubes 4 and 5 is also the same but is twice as high as in the first three.\n\nWhen all the balls are removed, which test tube will contain the least amount of water?",
    "image": "qimages/ukmt/pk-2025/q19.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "test tube 1"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "test tube 2"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "test tube 3"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "test tube 4"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "test tube 5"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Compare tubes at the same level",
        "body": "Tubes 1, 2 and 3 have the same level, but tube 3 has the most balls (3 against 1 and 2), so it has the least water. Likewise tube 5 has more balls than tube 4, so tube 5 has less water than tube 4. The least water is in tube 3 or tube 5.\n\nPour everything from two copies of tube 3 (water and balls) into one tube: you get a tube that looks exactly like tube 5, with twice the water. So tube 5 has twice as much water as tube 3.\n\n**Answer: (C) test tube 3**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Think in volumes",
        "body": "Let one ball have volume $b$ and tube 3 hold total volume $V$ below its water level, so the water in tube 3 is $V-3b$. Tube 5 reaches level $2V$ and holds 6 balls, so its water is $2V-6b=2(V-3b)$. So tube 5 has twice as much water as tube 3, and tube 3 has the least.\n\n**Answer: (C) test tube 3**"
      }
    ],
